{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790374220,"nonce":"rTKRhxKZYSopvak29TiwlvQgxB4=","title":"Generic call inference depends on declaration order (Issue9046)","message":"Probe (single file):\n\n    class Base { public function new() {}\n      function aeq\u003cT\u003e(e:Array\u003cT\u003e, a:Array\u003cT\u003e):Void trace(Std.string(e[0]) == Std.string(a[0])); }\n    class Q1 extends Base {\n      function test() { var a = U.flatten('hello'); aeq(['hello'], a); }\n      static function main() new Q1().test(); }\n    class U { public static function flatten\u003cT\u003e(i:T):Array\u003cT\u003e return [i]; }\n\n`aeq` is trap-stubbed ([trap-stub:generic] Base.aeq; mono: \"aeq NO explicit type_args\"). Moving class U ABOVE Q1 makes it pass. @:generic on flatten is irrelevant.\n\nScoping a method's own type parameters during signature pre-registration (classes.rs) did not change it, so the forward result type of `U.flatten` is not coming from that pre-registration. Next: instrument infer_call_type_arguments at the aeq call to print both argument types and which early return fires.","files":null}]}